---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { contact } from '../site';

const hasAnyRoute = Boolean(contact.formUrl || contact.issuesUrl || contact.email);
---
<Base
  title="About — doubleem"
  description="Why the software here is free, what it collects, and who makes it."
>
  {/* publik's content page: a small eyebrow, a two-line display headline with a full
      stop, then prose at a comfortable measure, and one filled control at the end. The
      one thing not copied is the colour — publik's eyebrow is in its accent, and
      DESIGN.md § The kicker is explicit that an accent-coloured kicker is the single
      most recognisable tell of a generated page. It is --ink-soft here, like every
      other label on the site. */}
  <section class="wrap intro">
    <p class="label">About</p>
    <h1>Good software should not cost you your data.</h1>
    <p class="intro__lead">
      doubleem is one person putting their work somewhere people can find it. Software
      mostly, for now. What each thing does, and what it costs you, is written on its own
      page — because those answers differ, and a promise made for everything is a promise
      that gets broken.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* `wrap` and `prose` on one element fought each other: .wrap sets max-width 1140
      and margin-inline auto, .prose then narrows max-width to 66ch — and the auto
      margins centred THAT, so the body text sat 90px to the right of the headline
      above it. Two elements, one job each: .wrap holds the page measure, .prose sits
      at its left edge. publik keeps its whole content page on one left edge and this
      is why that reads as deliberate. */}
  <section class="wrap">
   <div class="prose about">
    <h2>Who makes this</h2>
    <p>
      One person. The work tends to start the same way — someone I know has a problem,
      and the thing that would solve it either does not exist or costs money they should
      not have to spend. The first app here, <a href="/apps/logger/">LOGGER</a>, was
      built with a factory worker in Korea who
      needed to check whether his payslip was right and could not find anything that would
      do it in a language he reads.
    </p>
    <p>
      What turns up here next is not planned. Tools, probably. Possibly writing. It gets
      listed when it works.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site collects</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing. No cookies, no analytics, no tracking script, no newsletter box, and no
      email address required to download anything. The pages load no third-party scripts
      at all — even the typeface is served from here rather than from Google. That is why
      you were never shown a consent banner: there is nothing to consent to.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two scripts on every page, and a third that one of them fetches. I
      would rather name them than let you find them. All of them are served from this
      domain, all of them only move things, and all of them read nothing about you and
      store nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/motion.js">/motion.js</a> tilts the phone on the front page towards your
      cursor and lights the edge of a card as you pass it. Sixty-one lines of code, and
      twice that again explaining why they are there.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/globe.js">/globe.js</a> paints nothing at all. It measures how wide this
      window is, whether you are holding a mouse, and whether your browser can draw 3D,
      and fetches a different thing depending on the answer. A window wider than
      1200&nbsp;pixels — or a narrower one with a mouse — takes two more files:
      <a href="/three.min.js">/three.min.js</a>, the rendering library that does the
      drawing, and <a href="/globe-scene.js">/globe-scene.js</a>, the scene itself. On
      the front page that scene is a planet, in the sky behind the story; on this page
      and every other one it is only the drift of lit specks you can see behind these
      words — the same field, with the planet left out.
    </p>
    <p>
      A phone takes <a href="/sky-mobile.js">/sky-mobile.js</a> instead: the same drift
      of specks, drawn in a plain 2D canvas, with no planet and no library behind it.
      That is about 20&nbsp;KB, and it is there so that a handset never downloads the
      589&nbsp;KB one — including a handset asking for the desktop site, which is why the
      line is drawn at 1200 and not at the width a phone claims in that mode. A tablet is
      on the other side of it and gets the planet. If you are counting requests: three
      scripts on a phone, four on anything wider, and the specks are the last thing to
      arrive and the first thing to go — underneath them the page is the same ground,
      glow and grid it would be if none of this turned up at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      three.js is somebody else's work, used under the MIT licence, and its copyright
      notice is at the top of the file. It is vendored into this site rather than loaded
      from a CDN, which is what keeps "no third-party scripts" a fact about the network
      and not just about intent: nothing on these pages is fetched from another domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of them <em>could</em> send anything anywhere if they tried: these pages set a
      Content-Security-Policy that grants no permission to contact a server, so the
      browser would refuse. Read them if you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      What each <em>app</em> collects is a separate question with a separate answer, and it
      is written on that app's own page. LOGGER, for instance, has no server at all.
      Another thing built for another purpose might, and if so it will say so plainly.
    </p>

    <h2>On money</h2>
    <p>
      The apps here are free. For LOGGER that is not only generosity — a free calculator
      that shows its arithmetic is a different thing under Korean law from paid labour
      consulting, and that distinction matters more than the money would.
    </p>
    <p>
      No paid tier, no "pro" version held back, nothing here that is a demo of something
      larger you have to buy.
    </p>

    <h2>Telling me something is wrong</h2>
    <p>
      If something here gives you a wrong number, I want to know. That matters more than
      anything else on this page — LOGGER works out wages, and a figure that is quietly
      wrong is worse than no figure at all. You do not need to be sure it is a bug. "This
      does not match my payslip" is enough to start from.
    </p>

    {hasAnyRoute ? (
      <ul class="routes">
        {contact.formUrl && (
          <li>
            <a href={contact.formUrl}>Report a problem</a> — a short form. No account, no
            sign-up, and it does not go anywhere public.
          </li>
        )}
        {contact.issuesUrl && (
          <li>
            <a href={contact.issuesUrl}>Open an issue on GitHub</a> — public and easier to
            track, if you already have an account.
          </li>
        )}
        {contact.email && (
          <li><a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a></li>
        )}
      </ul>
    ) : (
      <p>There is no way to reach me from this page yet. That is an omission, not a policy.</p>
    )}

    <h2>Why there are no comments or reviews here</h2>
    <p>
      There is nowhere on this site to leave a public comment or a star rating, and that is
      deliberate. Someone checking whether their employer has underpaid them should not
      have to attach their name to it in public, and the feedback worth having — <em>this
      number is wrong</em> — is exactly the kind nobody leaves in a review box.
    </p>
    <p>
      If praise ever appears on this site it will be something a real person said, quoted
      with their permission. Not a widget, and not a number I cannot show you the source of.
    </p>

    {/* One way out, at the end, the way publik ends its content pages. A page that
        explains the place should hand the reader the place. */}
    <p class="about__out">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/apps/">See the work <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </p>
   </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The header floats and no longer pushes anything down; this page has to clear it
     itself, the same way the hero and the app pages do. See --head-h in global.css. */
  .intro { padding-top: calc(var(--head-h) + clamp(16px, 3vw, 40px)); }
  .intro .label { margin-bottom: 18px; }
  .intro h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 17ch;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6vw, 64px);
    font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: var(--track-display); line-height: 1.02;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .intro__lead {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    max-width: 52ch;
    font-size: clamp(17px, 2vw, 21px);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .about { margin-top: clamp(34px, 5vw, 56px); }
  .about__out { margin-top: clamp(30px, 4vw, 44px); }

  .routes { margin: 0 0 1em; padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .routes li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .routes a { font-weight: 600; }
</style>
